"peer_ticker": "AVGO",
"peer_disclosure_id": "avgo_2024_1A_D057",
"relationship": "same_topic",
"comparison": "Both MU and AVGO identify operational disruptions, including those caused by geopolitical turmoil, as risks to their supply and operations. MU discloses that suppliers could be affected by 'regional conflicts, civil unrest... sanctions, tariffs' which could limit material supply or increase costs (mu_2024_1A_P048_S001). AVGO explicitly lists 'geopolitical turmoil, trade tensions... political unrest, military conflicts' as events that could cause prolonged disruptions at manufacturing facilities, limiting capacity to meet customer demand (avgo_2024_1A_P057_S001). While MU's disclosure focuses on the supply side (materials/equipment availability), AVGO's focuses on facility-level capacity constraints, both cite geopolitical factors as a primary driver of supply chain instability.",
